          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            and in the theory of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamical+system"} dynamical system
            %strong observable
            is a quantity in some theoretical framework whose value can be measured and observed in principle. Any good theoretical framework of physical phenomena should come with carefully established notion of an observable.
          %h2#in_classical_physics In classical physics
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            an
            %strong observable
            is any smooth function on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of the system, and of time. The value of the observable is just the value of the function for fixed argument.
          %h2#in_quantum_physics In quantum physics
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            an
            %strong observable
            is a Hermitean operator on the physical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            of the theory. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observable"} quantum observable
            for more details.
          %p In this case, one distinguishes the concepts of the expectation value of the observable and the concept of the measured value; they are evaluated in some state of the system. The expectation value can be taken in any state of the system, while the measured value is always in some eigenstate of the observable operator. The process of measurement results in the quantum mechanical collapse or reduction, in which the system passes to an eigenstate of the measured operator. The probability of taking a given eigenstate depends on the the transition matrix element from the previously prepared state to the given eigenstate.
          %h2#in_quantum_field_theory In quantum field theory
          %p
            In relativistic quantum mechanics and relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            the question of observables is more complicated: issues like causality and superselection sectors are involved.
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            approach to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            the system of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            localized in given spacetime regions are the very foundation of the theory, called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            for QFT).
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            are meant to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"}> perturbative AQFT
            ) they are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+algebras"} formal power series algebras
          %div
            %p
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                of
